### CHART — running a query chart and asserting its data
###
### This file lints cleanly with no backend. It demonstrates the `CHART` verb and the
### `EXPECT Chart` / `EXPECT Chart.Data` subjects.
###
### `CHART "<name>"` runs the server's `QueryFilter.Chart` system action for the named chart of the
### current query — the same call the web client's dashboard makes — and CAPTURES the returned chart
### JSON. Unlike ACTION, a chart is a read-only observable of the query, NOT a navigation destination:
###   • no frame is pushed (the current query stays current), and
###   • the capture is cleared by the next executable verb, like the per-verb ClientOperation buffer.
###
### Read the result with `EXPECT Chart.Data` (the aggregated chart JSON — assert with CONTAINS / MATCHES
### / =) or the bare `EXPECT Chart` for a presence check (IS NULL / IS NOT NULL). A chart name the server
### doesn't know fails the verb loudly with the server's "Missing chart <name>" notification.
###
### The live round-trip is exercised by Vidyano.Script.IntegrationTests (ChartTests), whose in-process
### model registers a "ByColor" bar chart via ProductActions.OnChart.

@app = "https://demo.vidyano.com"

SIGN-IN admin / admin

### Run a named chart of the current query and assert on the returned JSON. No nav frame is pushed —
### the Products query is still current afterwards.
OPEN MenuItem Products
CHART "ByColor"
EXPECT NavStack.Top.Kind = "Query"                       ## chart pushed nothing; still on the list
EXPECT Chart.Data CONTAINS "barchart"
EXPECT Chart.Data MATCHES "\"name\":\"Blue\",\"value\":2"

### The capture is per-verb: once another verb runs, the stale chart is gone.
SEARCH "Widget"
EXPECT Chart IS NULL

### A leading Detail "<name>" clause runs the chart against a named detail query on the current PO,
### mirroring ACTION Detail "<name>".
OPEN MenuItem ProductCategories
OPEN-ROW WHERE Name = "Tools"
CHART Detail "ProductCategory_Products" "ByColor"
EXPECT Chart.Data CONTAINS "barchart"

### An unknown chart name is refused server-side — the run fails on the "Missing chart" notification.
### (Shown here as a comment so the sample stays green; uncomment against a live server to see it fail.)
### CHART "NoSuchChart"
